  "body": "### What problem are you trying to solve?\n\nOver at [sanitizer-api](https://github.com/WICG/sanitizer-api/), we deal a lot with user-supplied maps where a given key may be absent. [Get the value of an entry](https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#map-get) asserts that a key is present, which requires to first check for the key presence. This gets quite \"wordy\" quickly, especially if accesses are chained.\n\n\n### What solutions exist today?\n\nToday, one can always add an if-then:\n\n* If *a* *contains* *b*: \n  * ... do stuff with *a[b]* ...\n  * Otherwise, handle default case.\n\nChained case:\n* If *map* *contains* *a*:\n  *  If *map[a]* *contains* *b*:\n      * ... do stuff with *map[a][b]*.\n      * Otherwise, return *default*.\n  * Otherwise, return *default*.\n\n### How would you solve it?\n\nIn https://github.com/WICG/sanitizer-api/pull/285, I added a definition [\"maybe get the value of an entry\"](https://pr-preview.s3.amazonaws.com/otherdaniel/purification/pull/285.html#map-maybe-get-the-value), which basically extends infra's *get the value of an entry* with a default parameter, which it will return if a key isn't set. Plus a two-parameter shorthand, *map[key,default]*.\n\nExamples:\n- Safely accessing a map: *map[\"a\", None]*\n- Safely accessing a map of maps: *map[\"a\",«[]»][\"b\", default]*\n\nDefinition plus usage examples:\nhttps://pr-preview.s3.amazonaws.com/otherdaniel/purification/pull/285.html#map-maybe-get-the-value\n\nPrevious versions of the same PR use the existing syntax with if-guards.\n\n\n### Anything else?\n\n_No response_",
